02294921875, 0.06060791015625, 0.0184326171875, -0.004756927490234375, -0.0095977783203125, -0.0338134765625, 0.0035419464111328125, -0.0352783203125, -0.0158843994140625, 0.010162353515625, 0.036865234375, 0.038970947265625, 0.00971221923828125, 0.0081787109375, 0.0303802490234375, 0.0103607177734375, -0.04864501953125, -0.0033111572265625, 0.0012502670288085938, 0.0307769775390625, -0.005252838134765625, -0.01105499267578125, 0.01377105712890625, -0.0200042724609375, -0.05047607421875, 0.0343017578125, 0.00699615478515625, -0.005184173583984375, 0.007030487060546875, -0.01593017578125, -0.0245361328125, 0.0166778564453125, -0.020538330078125, -0.02008056640625, 0.0187835693359375, 0.0183868408203125, 0.0208282470703125, 0.01548004150390625, -0.031982421875, -0.01326751708984375, 0.00913238525390625, 0.0201873779296875, -0.0040130615234375, 0.00971221923828125, 0.01045989990234375, -0.01555633544921875, 0.0233917236328125, -0.007465362548828125, 0.003360748291015625, 0.04052734375, -0.0159759521484375, 0.0018167495727539062, 0.019378662109375], "payload": {"model": "text-embedding-3-small", "text": "Recent research has explored the application of Geometric Algebra (GA) to the modeling, analysis, and control of dynamical systems, particularly in electrical engineering and physics. Many problems in electromagnetics, robotics, and control theory involve quantities such as rotations, bivectors, or coupled vector fields that are naturally and compactly represented as multivector-valued functions. For example, in the analysis of three-phase electrical machines, the state variables are best described as elements of a Clifford algebra, and in rigid body dynamics, the orientation and angular velocity are naturally encoded as rotors and bivectors, respectively. In such cases, the classical Laplace transform, which acts on scalar- or vector-valued functions, is inadequate for directly analyzing systems where the unknowns or inputs are multivectors. Extending the Laplace transform to multivector-valued functions enables direct application of powerful integral transform techniques to these problems, facilitating solution, stability analysis, and control design in a manner that respects the underlying geometric structure. For instance, in the control of three-phase AC machines, the use of GA allows for a more compact and coordinate-free representation of the system dynamics, while in electromagnetic field analysis, Maxwell\u2019s equations can be written in a single GA equation, but their solution often requires integral transforms that act on multivector fields. Traditional Laplace transform methods require decomposing such systems into scalar or vector components, losing geometric information and increasing computational complexity. The proposed extension allows for direct analysis and synthesis in the native multivector setting, preserving geometric relationships and potentially simplifying both theoretical and computational aspects.\n\n**Roadmap and Structure**  \nThis document is organized as follows. Section 1 introduces the necessary definitions and notation for geometric algebras and multivector-valued functions. Section 2 discusses the function spaces relevant for the Laplace transform, clarifies the choice of norm, and analyzes the implications for existence, uniqueness, and inversion. Section 3 defines the Laplace transform in the GA setting, justifies the restriction to scalar Laplace parameters, and systematically analyzes the mathematical obstacles to more general definitions, including formal statements and proofs. Section 4 provides a concrete, fully worked example illustrating the application and advantages of the proposed transform. Section 5 reviews and critically compares related work, discusses inversion and uniqueness theorems, and addresses computational aspects, including algorithmic considerations. Section 6 clarifies the scope and dimensionality of our results.\n\n**1. Definitions and Notation**  \nLet \\(\\mathcal{G}(p,q)\\) denote the real geometric (Clifford) algebra of signature \\((p,q)\\), generated by an orthonormal basis \\(\\{e_1, \\ldots, e_{p+q}\\}\\) with \\(e_i^2 = 1\\) for \\(1 \\leq i \\leq p\\) and \\(e_i^2 = -1\\) for \\(p < i \\leq p+q\\). A *multivector* in \\(\\mathcal{G}(p,q)\\) is a linear combination of basis blades (elements of different grades). A *multivector-valued function* is a function \\(f: [0, \\infty) \\to \\mathcal{G}(p,q)\\), i.e., for each \\(t \\geq 0\\), \\(f(t)\\) is a multivector. The *exponential map* for a multivector \\(A\\) is defined by the power series \\(e^{A} = \\sum_{k=0}^\\infty \\frac{A^k}{k!}\\), but closed forms are generally available only for special cases (e.g., blades or bivectors).\n\nWe use the Euclidean (Hilbert-Schmidt) norm for multivectors, defined as \\(\\|f(t)\\| = \\left( \\sum_A |f_A(t)|^2 \\right)^{1/2}\\), where \\(f(t) = \\sum_A f_A(t) A\\) is the expansion in the basis blades \\(A\\). This norm is standard in the literature, is submultiplicative, and ensures that convergence and integrability properties are well-defined for the component functions. The choice of this norm is motivated by its compatibility with the inner product structure of \\(\\mathcal{G}(p,q)\\) and its ability to control the growth of all components simultaneously. Alternative norms, such as the maximum norm or \\(L^p\\) norms for \\(p \\neq 2\\), could be considered, but the Hilbert-Schmidt norm is most natural for analysis and inversion, as it allows the use of Parseval-type identities and is compatible with standard functional analysis results.\n\n**2. Function Spaces, Existence, and Uniqueness**  \nWe define the Laplace transform for multivector-valued functions \\(f: [0, \\infty) \\to \\mathcal{G}(p,q)\\) as follows. Let \\(L^1([0, \\infty), \\mathcal{G}(p,q))\\) denote the space of functions for which each real coefficient function (in the basis expansion) is Lebesgue integrable on \\([0, \\infty)\\). That is, if \\(f(t) = \\sum_{A} f_A(t) A\\) (sum over basis blades \\(A\\)), then \\(f_A \\in L^1([0, \\infty), \\mathbb{R})\\) for all \\(A\\). This ensures that the Laplace integral converges for each component. The space \\(L^2([0, \\infty), \\mathcal{G}(p,q))\\) consists of functions for which \\(\\int_0^\\infty \\|f(t)\\|^2 dt < \\infty\\). Clearly, \\(L^2 \\subset L^1\\) for functions vanishing at infinity, but not in general. For Laplace transforms, \\(L^1\\) is the minimal requirement for the existence of the transform as an improper integral. For functions with faster than exponential growth, the Laplace transform may not exist; for functions in \\(L^2\\) or smoother spaces, additional properties such as uniqueness and inversion can be established using standard results from harmonic analysis. In particular, if \\(f \\in L^1\\) and \\(\\|f(t)\\| \\leq M e^{\\alpha t}\\), then the Laplace transform exists for \\(\\operatorname{Re}(s) > \\alpha\\). Uniqueness of the Laplace transform follows from the uniqueness of the Laplace transform for each component function. Inversion is guaranteed under the same conditions as in the scalar case: if \\(F(s)\\) is analytic in a right half-plane and of exponential type, then the inverse Laplace transform recovers \\(f(t)\\) almost everywhere. For a rigorous treatment, see [Widder, \"The Laplace Transform,\" 1941].\n\n**3. Definition of the Laplace Transform in GA and Mathematical Obstacles**  \nFor \\(f \\in L^1([0, \\infty), \\mathcal{G}(p,q))\\) and Laplace parameter \\(s \\in \\mathbb{C}\\) with \\(\\operatorname{Re}(s) > s_0\\) (for some \\(s_0\\) depending on the growth of \\(f\\)), we define\n\\[\n\\mathcal{L}[f](s) = \\int_0^\\infty e^{-st} f(t) \\, dt,\n\\]\nwhere \\(e^{-st}\\) is the standard scalar exponential, which commutes with all multivectors in the integrand. This ensures that the integral is well-defined, linear, and that the Laplace transform retains classical properties such as linearity and shift invariance. The transform exists and is unique for all \\(f\\) such that \\(\\|f(t)\\|\\) grows at most exponentially as \\(t \\to \\infty\\).\n\n**Obstacles to Generalizing the Laplace Parameter**\n\n*Theorem 1 (Non-commutativity):*  \nLet \\(s \\in \\mathcal{G}(p,q)\\) be a non-scalar multivector and \\(f(t)\\) a multivector-valued function. Then, in general, \\(e^{-st} f(t) \\neq f(t) e^{-st}\\).  \n*Proof:*  \nLet \\(s = e_1\\), \\(f(t) = e_2\\) in \\(\\mathcal{G}(2,0)\\). Then\n\\[\ne^{-e_1 t} e_2 = (\\cosh t - e_1 \\sinh t) e_2 = e_2 \\cosh t - (e_1 e_2) \\sinh t,\n\\]\nwhile\n\\[\ne_2 e^{-e_1 t} = e_2 (\\cosh t - e_1 \\sinh t) = e_2 \\cosh t - (e_2 e_1) \\sinh t.\n\\]\nSince \\(e_1 e_2 = -e_2 e_1\\), the two expressions differ by a sign in the bivector term. This non-commutativity implies that the order of multiplication in the Laplace integral is ambiguous, and classical properties such as linearity and shift invariance may fail.\n\n*Theorem 2 (Lack of Closed Forms and Convergence):*  \nThe exponential map \\(e^{-st}\\) for general \\(s \\in \\mathcal{G}(p,q)\\) may not have a closed form or converge for all \\(t\\). For blades or bivectors, closed forms exist (see [Ablamowicz & Fauser, 2004]), but for general multivectors, the power"}}